# ARBITER — environment
#
# Copy to `.env` (which is gitignored) and edit. Every value below has a working
# default, so an EMPTY .env — or none at all — is a valid configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   npm install && npm run seed:demo && npm run dev
#
# Hand out THIS file, never your own `.env`.
#
# THREE NAMES ARE READ, in order: `.env`, then `.env.share`, then `.env.defaults`.
#
#   .env           yours, gitignored.    Your own credentials and overrides.
#   .env.share     handed to you, gitignored. Exists so a configuration prepared for
#                  somebody else works where it lands, instead of needing a rename step
#                  whose only real function was to be forgotten — a share file sitting
#                  unread looks exactly like having no credentials at all.
#   .env.defaults  TRACKED IN GIT. The settings the team has agreed on and that are not
#                  secret: which models, and whether the demonstration team and the four
#                  library cases are created on boot. Never a credential — this
#                  repository is public.
#
# LAYERED BY NAME, not by file. Each file sets only the names still unset, so a `.env`
# holding nothing but your own API key overrides that one name and inherits the shared
# models from `.env.defaults` — it does not silently replace them. A blank value
# (`ARBITER_MODEL=`) counts as unset and falls through to the code default, which is how
# you drop a shared line without editing the tracked file.
#
# The real environment still beats all three: a shell export, a CI secret or a host's
# injected configuration wins over any file. The startup banner lists every file it read.
#
# MOST OF THIS FILE IS NOW REDUNDANT for ordinary use — `.env.defaults` already carries
# the shared configuration. What is left here is the reference for every name that
# exists, and the place to start from when you want your own credentials instead.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. NOTHING AT ALL — the default, and it works
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# With no credentials the product runs. Cases, positions, blind reveal, unanimity,
# the audit and the hash-chained record are pure code and need no model.
#
#   Adjudication  runs against a stub, and every response carries `source: "stub"`
#                 so it can never be read as a model's answer (services/api/probe.ts).
#   Ask & summary return 503 {"error":"no_key"}. These are the only surfaces that
#                 genuinely require a model.
#
# The startup banner tells you which of the two you are in. Leave sections 2 and 3
# blank unless you want live AI.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. LIVE AI — pick ONE provider. Server-side only; never sent to a browser.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The provider is inferred from the MODEL NAME (services/api/interpret.ts): a name
# starting `gemini-` routes to Vertex, anything else to Anthropic. There is no second
# switch to disagree with, so no pair of variables can name a model its provider
# cannot serve.

# --- 2a. Anthropic: the lower-friction path. A key, and nothing else. ---
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# ARBITER_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5

# --- 2b. Gemini: the project's own default. Three ways to authenticate. ---
#
# ADC is the default and the right one for a laptop you own. Each person runs, once:
#     gcloud auth application-default login
#     gcloud services enable aiplatform.googleapis.com
# and sets their OWN project below. Nothing secret goes in this file.
# ARBITER_GCP_PROJECT=your-gcp-project-id

# A KEY, when the model has to be shared. ADC authenticates a PERSON, so it cannot be
# handed to a team - everyone would need their own account on the project. A key is one
# line, and it is still a cloud credential: an `AQ.`-prefixed key is bound to a Google
# Cloud project and bills it. Sufficient on its own; needs no project id beside it.
#
# Sharing one key means sharing one budget, so ARBITER_MODEL_BUDGET below matters more
# once this is in use, not less.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=AQ....

# Which service the key talks to. The two serve DIFFERENT model catalogues, so this is
# typed rather than inferred - `vertex` is the default and the catalogue this project's
# numbers were measured on.
#   vertex     aiplatform.googleapis.com, express mode. Needs aiplatform.googleapis.com
#              enabled on the key's project or every call is a 403 naming that project.
#   developer  generativelanguage.googleapis.com. Needs no project setup at all, but it
#              does not serve gemini-2.5-flash-lite, so a deployment here must set
#              ARBITER_MODEL to something it does serve.
# ARBITER_GEMINI_HOST=vertex

# Alternatives to ADC, for a deployed service rather than a laptop. On Google Cloud
# prefer none of them: attach a service account to the service and the library finds it
# with no key material anywhere. GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_JSON must be ONE line -
# the loader is line-based, and a pretty-printed blob loads as garbage.
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/absolute/path/to/service-account.json
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS_JSON={"type":"service_account", ...}

# Model overrides. Defaults: gemini-3.5-flash for adjudication/ask/summary,
# gemini-2.5-flash-lite for short classification calls.
# ARBITER_MODEL=
# ARBITER_ADJUDICATION_MODEL=
# ARBITER_ASK_MODEL=

# Consensus runs per adjudication, capped at 9 in runsFrom(). Each run is a separate
# billed call, so this multiplies spend directly.
# ARBITER_ADJUDICATION_RUNS=3

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. SERVING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Bind address. Loopback unless you say otherwise, because this process terminates
# no TLS — set 0.0.0.0 only behind a proxy that does.
# ARBITER_HOST=127.0.0.1

# API port. Most hosts inject PORT themselves.
# PORT=8787

# Where the two Vite dev servers proxy /api to, and the public dev port.
# API_PORT=8787
# ARBITER_PORT=5173

# Model calls one account may make per 10 minutes. This is the spend cap: it is what
# makes the paid endpoints safe to expose at all. Per-source is 6x this.
# ARBITER_MODEL_BUDGET=30

# Passages retrieved per Ask.
# ARBITER_ASK_K=

# Python executable used to measure and extract PDFs.
# PYTHON=python

# Create the five demonstration accounts on boot, if there are none. Equivalent to
# `npm run seed:demo`, and meant for a configuration handed to somebody who has not
# read the README: a product that opens on a sign-in screen with nobody to sign in as
# looks broken rather than unseeded.
#
# It runs ONLY into an empty account store, so it cannot appear beside real users and
# cannot resurrect a team that was deliberately deleted. The five share one published
# password. Leave this unset for anything holding real data.
# ARBITER_DEMO_SEED=1

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. BROWSER-EXPOSED — anything named VITE_* IS PUBLIC
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Vite compiles these into the client bundle. They are readable by anyone who loads
# the page. Never put a credential, a key, or a project id behind a VITE_ name to
# "make it available to the frontend" — that publishes it.
#
# VITE_AUTO_PASSWORD is already public for exactly this reason. It signs everyone in
# as one demo identity, which means the record says that person decided whoever was
# at the keyboard (see the note in apps/deliberation/src/App.tsx). Fine for a demo,
# not fine as attribution.

# VITE_AUTO_EMAIL=r.okafor@arbiter.demo
# VITE_AUTO_PASSWORD=arbiter-demo-2026
# VITE_SITE_URL=/
# VITE_APP_URL=/deliberation/

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. EVALUATION HARNESS — only for `npm run probe`
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PROBE_RUNS=20
# PROBE_CASE=data/probe-case.json
# PROBE_OUT=results/probe-runs.json
